{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Of their flesh you shall not eat.</b> I know only these. Whence do I derive other unclean beasts that have no sign of purity at all? You say a fortiori: now these, which have some signs of purity, are forbidden — all the more so those (Sifra)." "<b>Of their flesh.</b> Their flesh is under the prohibition, but not their bones, sinews, horns, and hooves (ibid.)." "<b>And in their carcasses you shall not touch.</b> One might think Israelites are warned against touching a carcass — Scripture says ״אֱמֹר אֶל הַכֹּהֲנִים וְגוֹ״״ (Leviticus 21): kohanim are warned, but Israelites are not warned — a fortiori, then: now if for corpse-impurity, which is severe, He warned only kohanim, for carcass-impurity, which is lighter, is it not all the more so? And what does Scripture teach by saying לֹא תִגָּעוּ? On account of habit (ibid.; Rosh HaShanah 16)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {2 "The last phrase — 'on account of habit' (בָּרֶגֶל) — means: the warning against touching is specifically during a festival pilgrimage, lest one handle the carcass to prepare it as food. Rosh HaShanah 16a is the Talmudic source."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:05:49.045954727Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}